By default, a fixture is considered part of a test, meaning its setup and teardown running time counts toward the overall test timeout.

To prevent a slow fixture from inflating the timeout for every test, you can set a separate, larger timeout specifically for that fixture. This allows you to keep the overall test timeout small while giving the slow fixture the necessary time to complete.

This is implemented by using the tuple syntax in `test.extend()`:

```typescript
const test = base.extend<{ slowFixture: string }>({
  slowFixture: [
    async ({}, use) => {
      // ... perform a slow operation ...
      await use('hello');
    },
    { timeout: 60000 }
  ]
});
```

