const test = base.extend<{ slowFixture: string }>({
  slowFixture: [async ({}, use) => {
    // ... perform a slow operation ...
    await use('hello');
  }, { timeout: 60000 }]
});

llm_response:
The cleanest fix is to set a separate timeout for that specific fixture. While fixtures share the test timeout by default, you can provide a separate timeout value for slow fixtures (especially worker-scoped ones) by defining it within the fixture configuration.

For example, when extending the test, you can specify the timeout in the fixture's configuration array:

```typescript
const test = base.extend<{ slowFixture: string }>({
  slowFixture: [
    async ({}, use) => {
      // ... perform a slow operation ...
      await use('hello');
    },
    { timeout: 60_000 } // Set a separate timeout for this fixture
  ],
});
```

This allows you to give the slow fixture more time while keeping the overall test timeout small.

